---
source: tests/stages.rs
expression: reports.report(path)
input_file: tests/fixtures/expand/doc_syntax_pages.knv
---
(def thread-page "(->> value form ...)\n\nThreads a value through each form as its last argument.\n\n`(->> 3 (- 10) (* 2))` is `(* 2 (- 10 3))`. A bare name in place of a form\nis called with the value alone.\n\n(->> 3 (- 10) (* 2))\n=> 14\n\n(->> (range 0 3) collect)\n=> (vec 0 1 2)")
(def the-page "(the type value)\n\nGives a value a written type.\n\nIt binds the value with an annotated `let` and returns it, so a literal is\nchecked against the type and a value that is not `copy` moves. A value of\nanother type is an error, except that a borrowed `string`, `(ref name)`,\npasses as a `(ref str)`.\n\n(the i64 3)\n=> 3\n\n(local word (str \"pear\"))\n(the (ref str) (ref word))\n=> \"pear\"")
(def loop-page "(loop (name value ...) body ...)\n\nBinds names to values and runs a body that `recur` repeats.\n\nThe list is the binding list of `let`, so each value sees the names bound\nbefore it. A name given twice is an error, because `recur` passes one value\nper name.\n\n`(recur new-value ...)` in tail position runs the body again with each name\nbound to its new value. The call returns the body's last value.\n\nThe body is a function, so `try`, `let-try` and `try->` in it leave the loop\nwith the failure, not the enclosing function.\n\n(loop (total 0\n      n 3)\n  (if (> n 0) (recur (+ total n) (- n 1)) total))\n=> 6")
(def let-match-page "(let-match (pattern value ...) body ...)\n\nBinds the names of each pattern to the parts of its value, in order.\n\nThe list is the binding list of `let`, with a `match` pattern in place of\neach name, so `(let-match ((pair word count) entry) body ...)` binds `word`\nand `count`. Each value sees the names bound before it. Each pattern is a\none-arm `match`, so a pattern that some value fails, such as `(some x)`, is\nan error that names the missing case. On an owned value, each name moves its\npart unless the part is `copy`, and `_` leaves its part in place.\n\n(let-match ((pair word total) (pair \"pear\" 3))\n  (str word total))\n=> \"pear3\"")
(def let-try-page "(let-try (name value ...) body ...)\n\nBinds each name to the value inside a `result` or an `option`, in order.\n\nThe list is the binding list of `let`.\n\nEach value goes through `try`, so the first `err` or `none` returns from the\nenclosing function, or leaves the enclosing `loop`. That function must\nreturn a `result` or an `option` of the same kind.\n\n(local step (fn ((value (option i64))) (let-try (n value) (some (+ n 1)))))\n(step (some 1))\n=> (some 2)\n\n(step none)\n=> none")
(def try-thread-page "(try-> value function ...)\n\nCalls each function on the result of the one before, through `try`.\n\n`(try-> x f g)` is `(g (try (f x)))`, so the first `err` or `none` returns\nfrom the enclosing function, or leaves the enclosing `loop`, and the last\nresult is returned as it is. With no function, the call returns the value.\n\nUnlike `->`, a step takes no extra arguments: a call form is a call whose\nresult is the function, so `(try-> x (make-step))` is `((make-step) x)`.\n\n(local half (fn ((n i64)) (if (> n 0) (some (/ n 2)) none)))\n(local twice-halved (fn ((n i64)) (try-> n half half)))\n(twice-halved 8)\n=> (some 2)\n\n(twice-halved 0)\n=> none")
(def comment-page "(comment form ...)\n\nKeeps forms in the source that never expand, check or run.\n\nThe call is unit, so a file can hold scratch expressions to evaluate one at\na time from an editor.\n\n(comment (never checked))\n=> (do) ...")
